= Dunning–Kruger effect — Simple =

<onlyinclude>The '''Dunning–Kruger effect''' refers to research on how people judge their own performance or ability, particularly the claim that people performing poorly in a domain may overestimate their competence and fail to recognize mistakes. Justin Kruger and David Dunning reported such patterns in 1999. However, the usual popular chart and the idea that all beginners are extremely confident are misleading: researchers dispute how much of the original pattern reflects metacognitive skill rather than statistical artifacts and measurement choices.<ref name="original">Justin Kruger and David Dunning, [https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.77.6.1121 “Unskilled and unaware of it: How difficulties in recognizing one's own incompetence lead to inflated self-assessments”], ''Journal of Personality and Social Psychology'' 77(6), 1121–1134 (1999).</ref><ref name="critique">Gilles E. Gignac and Marcin Zajenkowski, [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.intell.2020.101449 “The Dunning–Kruger effect is (mostly) a statistical artefact”], ''Intelligence'' 80 (2020), competing interpretation.</ref>

== What were the original questions? ==

Researchers compared people's performance on tasks with their own estimates of how well they had done. The original paper examined tasks such as logical reasoning, grammar and humor judgments.

One interpretation was that weaker performers lack some skills needed to recognize their own errors. But differences between actual scores and self-ratings can also reflect uncertain measurements, regression toward the average and people generally believing they performed well.

A conclusion about the mechanism requires more than a graph of low and high scorers.

== A simple example ==

Imagine a test on which a person's observed score is unusually low, partly because they had a bad day. Their self-rating reflects their longer-term skill but not that day's temporary problem. Their self-rating may be higher than that observed score even if they have no special tendency to boast or misjudge themselves.

Likewise, a person who happened to score unusually high might rate themselves below that one-time result. This is one reason researchers must separate genuine insight from measurement noise.

== What is the famous confidence curve? ==

Internet diagrams often show confidence soaring to a peak called “Mount Stupid” and then sinking. That exact curve is not a scientifically established result of the original paper. The studies used actual test performance and judgments, not a universal confidence-versus-experience graph.

A novice may know that they are a novice. Experts may sometimes be overconfident. The phenomenon should not be treated as a personality label or an insult.

== What would better evidence look like? ==

A useful study measures a person's ability repeatedly, uses reliable tests, records estimates before feedback and compares several possible explanations for errors in self-judgment.

Some studies support metacognitive explanations; others highlight statistical factors that reproduce familiar graphs without assuming poorer insight among weak performers.<ref name="stat">[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8992690/ “A Statistical Explanation of the Dunning–Kruger Effect”], ''Frontiers in Psychology'' (2022), formal alternative account.</ref>

== What can we use the idea for? ==

We can encourage people to seek feedback, test predictions and update their beliefs. But the evidence does not license diagnosing strangers' ability from how confidently they speak.

[[Metacognition]], [[Cognition]] and [[Reasoning]] are related areas of study.

